+# Research
+
+## Request paths and files
+
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/order/OrderApi.java`
+  owns all three admin endpoints. `GET /private/orders/{id}` delegates to `OrderFacade.getReadableOrder`; `GET /private/orders/customers/{id}` first loads a `Customer` and returns HTTP 404 when it is absent; the paginated admin list builds an `OrderCriteria` and delegates to the facade.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/controller/order/facade/OrderFacadeImpl.java`
+  converts core orders to the v0 admin response. The customer-scoped method passes `customerId` into the criteria. The criteria-based admin method calls `orderService.getOrders`, but returns an object with `recordsTotal=0` whenever the current page is empty, losing the count and page total. Single-order conversion tries to populate a customer only when the customer still exists, while the order populator retains billing, delivery, and placed email.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepository.java`
+  has the single-order query. Several `join fetch` clauses are inner joins, including products, totals, history, and product attributes/downloads; any missing related row can therefore make an otherwise retained order disappear.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepositoryImpl.java`
+  contains both list implementations. The admin criteria path is `listOrders`; the customer/admin legacy path is `listByStore`. Both filter by the scalar `o.customerId`, not by a customer relationship. `listOrders` orders newest first and applies filters, but uses `RepositoryHelper.paginateQuery` for page offsets and computes pages as `(count / pageSize) + 1`, which produces an incorrect extra page for exact multiples. `listByStore` is legacy and has no robust empty-page metadata handling.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/utils/RepositoryHelper.java`
+  calculates page offsets correctly for zero-based pages, but its page count is `(count / size) + 1`; it also only applies the new pagination branch when callers explicitly disable legacy pagination.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/order/Order.java`
+  deliberately stores `CUSTOMER_ID` as a nullable `Long` with the comment that an order can outlive a deleted customer. It separately stores `customerEmailAddress`, embedded billing, and embedded delivery, so historical order details do not require a live customer.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/customer/CustomerServiceImpl.java` and `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/controller/customer/facade/CustomerFacadeImpl.java`
+  delete the customer entity and attributes only; they do not delete or rewrite orders. This is compatible with retaining orders and permits a later registration with the same email to receive a new customer id.
+
+## Causes
+
+1. The customer-scoped endpoint treats a missing customer as an error before querying orders, so retained orders for a deleted customer can never be listed.
+2. The single-order query’s inner fetch joins can filter out a retained order at the database query level. The facade itself already tolerates a missing customer and leaves the response customer unset, but it cannot do so if the query returns no row.
+3. The admin list’s metadata is discarded for an empty page in `OrderFacadeImpl`; the response therefore reports zero totals instead of the matching count. Pagination metadata also has an off-by-one calculation in `RepositoryHelper`.
+4. The customer-scoped legacy conversion similarly returns an empty response without preserving the matching total, and its result query has a legacy pagination implementation that is less consistent with the required zero-based `page` contract.
+
+## Affected behavior
+
+- Admin order details, status updates, order history, and payment transaction reads all depend on resolving the order by id; the retained order must remain queryable without requiring customer existence.
+- Customer order lookup must be based on the stored order customer id and merchant store, not on the current customer row.
+- Re-registration safety is already provided by the order’s scalar customer id and the customer delete implementation; no email-based order lookup or reassignment must be introduced.
+- The response model is the deprecated v0 `ReadableOrderList`, whose `recordsTotal` and `totalPages` fields are consumed by the admin UI. Empty pages must still carry both totals and an empty (non-null where practical) order list.
+
